<p>I mentioned I <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/06/01/a-good-weekend.html">picked up Subnautica again</a>, and it&rsquo;s more fun than I remember it was <a href="https://taonaw.com/2019/01/16/of-subnautica-and-fear.html">my last time</a> with it. Why? Because I&rsquo;m kind of cheating. Hang on, hang on, hear me out&hellip;.</p>
<p>Subnautica is not an easy game for the easily distracted. For those of us who constantly forget what we were doing because there&rsquo;s a new shiny thing to focus on, Subnautica can be brutal. A large part of it is survival and crafting, which means my internal monologue goes something like this: &ldquo;I need some copper for the&hellip; oh wait, if I get more silver I can craft the wiring kit for that torpedo, but I need aerogel&hellip; what do I need for aerogel? Oh, gel sacks, right, OK, where&rsquo;s my Seamoth, ha, I forgot I docked it outside instead of the moonpool&hellip; oh crap oxygen! swim up! up up up up&hellip;! Whew! Wait, where am I? Where&rsquo;s the damn Seamoth? Ah! Here you are&hellip; good, now what was I doing? Silver? Or was it gold? I wanted gold for the CPU so I can craft&hellip; oh right! I need copper.&rdquo;</p>
<p>The game&rsquo;s a lot of fun, and it rewards you for exploring, but when your brain constantly works like the above, you tend to miss a lot. This is why I started looking online for things like where to find X, and watching a few helpful YouTube videos. I also learned which plants I can grow around my base so I can craft what I need more easily, instead of running around and forgetting what I&rsquo;m looking for. Because I played about 2/3 of the game before, I know the basic plot and what&rsquo;s going on, so I&rsquo;m taking my time now preparing for each new horrifying level and enjoying building my base and putting more details into it.</p>
